e handling of numbers (fuel calculations, etc). Weak memory for numbers could be a safety risk. So we could shift to C, who has strong situational awareness and leadership, though unfriendly. But we need to consider C's unfriendly behavior and not open to new ideas. That also is a concern.\n\nWe could propose that given B's weak numeric memory and interpersonal remarks, C might be a better fit if his unfriendly behavior is less impactful. But we need evidence on C's crew cooperation. So we could ask for that.\n\nThus we can change our vote to Candidate C, explaining why B's numeric memory weakness and remarks are concerning, and C's strengths outweigh his concerns, pending clarification on his unfriendly behavior.\n\nWe need to reference prior discussion: Agent 1 asked for concrete observations on B's remarks, A's communication, D's arrogance. Agent 3 asked for clarification on B's remarks and C's unfriendly behavior. So we can say: I agree we need more info, and I want to highlight B's weak numeric memory as an additional concern, which may affect attention and accuracy. Therefore I am shifting my provisional recommendation to Candidate C, assuming his unfriendly demeanor does not critically impair crew cooperation.\n\nWe must include explicit facts: B weak memory for numbers, B makes nasty remarks, C anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, regarded as show\u2011off, not open to new ideas.\n\nWe also need to ask for concrete examples of C's unfriendly behavior and its impact.\n\nThus produce public message and metadata with vote \"Candidate C\"."}
